<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of La Marck</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as La Marck family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of La Marck was a noble family from the County of Mark in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, one of whose branches rose to power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> from the 15th century. Its members gained control of the Duchy of Bouillon and the principality of Sedan, which they ruled as near-independent lords. Through the marriage of the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-de-la-marck">Charlotte de La Marck</a>, these lands passed to Henri de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne">La Tour d'Auvergne</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck.jpg" alt="House of La Marck" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_House_of_La_Marck.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the mid-15th century the <b>La Marck family</b> gained control of it, and by the 1480s they ruled it in practice as an independent duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bouillon">Duchy of Bouillon</a></li><li><blockquote>Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne, a Huguenot leader, married into the house of <b>La Marck</b> and became prince of Sedan.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-turenne">Viscounty of Turenne</a></li></ul></section>
